  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Drier conditions with seasonable temperatures \nand lower humidity Sunday into early next week.\n\nBroad high pressure over the Great Lakes will track over the \nMid-Atlantic through Mon. This will bring a change in airmass \nwith notably lower dew points. Highs in the low 80s for most \ntoday and Mon. Overnight lows in the 50s and low 60s.\n\nSfc high pressure begins to transition south of the Mid-\nAtlantic on Tue as upper level ridging builds over the \nSE. Temps likely begin to creep up again during this time, \naccompanied by increasing humidity as mid-level dry air tracks \nS of the region with more humid air coming in from the N. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Shower and thunderstorm chances return by mid \nweek.\n\nUpper-level ridging over the Appalachians starts to flatten into \nzonal flow beginning Wed. As this occurs, a trough forming over\nQuebec will begin to track east across NE, with a stemming cold\nfront moving across the East Coast by Thu. Temps gradually \nincrease during this time, along with increasing humidity ahead \nof the front. By Thu, temps potentially reach the lower 90s \nacross the metros. \n\nAs this frontal boundary tracks through the Mid-Atlantic, additional \nshower & t-storm activity is possible across the region \nbeginning Wed evening and into Thu. As of the 00Z model runs, \nthe GFS and ECMWF both indicate a Thu afternoon time of arrival\nfor most precip, but the exact coverage and nature of \nprecip remains uncertain. Some diurnal t-storm development \nremains possible during this period given the increasing \ninstability in the atmosphere by this time.\n\nAs this cold front transitions offshore towards the end of next week,\ntemps drop again & conditions become much drier heading into \nthe weekend as high pressure moves in overhead.",
